  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "conjunctions: and/but/or/because/so",
      "title_form": "Conectores básicos: and, but, or, because, so",
      "title_target": "Basic Connectors: and, but, or, because, so",
      "description_md_form": "Los conectores unen ideas para que tu inglés suene natural. Aquí tienes los cinco más útiles:\n\n- **and** (y): une ideas similares. *I like coffee **and** tea.*\n- **but** (pero): muestra contraste. *I like coffee, **but** I don't like tea.*\n- **or** (o): ofrece opciones. *Coffee **or** tea?*\n- **because** (porque): da una razón. *I drink coffee **because** I'm tired.*\n- **so** (así que): muestra resultado. *I'm tired, **so** I sleep.*\n\nUsa estos conectores para hablar de tus gustos, emociones y rutinas. Son la base para conversaciones reales.",
      "description_md_target": "Connectors join ideas to make your English sound natural. Here are the five most useful ones:\n\n- **and**: joins similar ideas. *I like coffee **and** tea.*\n- **but**: shows contrast. *I like coffee, **but** I don't like tea.*\n- **or**: offers options. *Coffee **or** tea?*\n- **because**: gives a reason. *I drink coffee **because** I'm tired.*\n- **so**: shows a result. *I'm tired, **so** I sleep.*\n\nUse these connectors to talk about your likes, emotions, and routines. They are the base for real conversations.",
      "grammar_structure": "Idea 1 + [and/but/or] + Idea 2 | [because/so] + Idea 2",
      "conjugation_table": null,
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "exm_gex_001",
          "lexeme_id": "because_conj",
          "chunk_id": null,
          "grammar_slug": null,
          "text_target": "I like it because it is good.",
          "translation_form": "Me gusta porque es bueno.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "because"
          ],
          "audio_url": null,
          "reading_target": null
        },
        {
          "id": "exm_gex_002",
          "lexeme_id": "so_conj",
          "chunk_id": null,
          "grammar_slug": null,
          "text_target": "I am sad, so I sleep.",
          "translation_form": "Estoy triste, así que duermo.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "so"
          ],
          "audio_url": null,
          "reading_target": null
        }
      ],
      "callouts": [
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "text_md": "**and** y **but** van entre dos ideas. **because** y **so** conectan una razón con un resultado."
        },
        {
          "type": "common_error",
          "text_md": "En español decimos *'porque'* al final, pero en inglés **because** va al principio de la razón: *I sleep **because** I am tired.*"
        }
      ],
      "common_errors_es": [
        "No digas 'I like coffee and I like tea' si quieres decir 'Me gusta el café y el té'. Usa 'I like coffee and tea'.",
        "No confundas 'so' (así que) con 'very' (muy). 'I am so tired' significa 'Estoy muy cansado', pero 'I am tired, so I sleep' significa 'Estoy cansado, así que duermo'.",
        "No uses 'because' al final de la oración como en español. En inglés, la razón va después de 'because': 'I am happy because I like it'."
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "common adverbs: also/too/very/really/just/maybe",
      "title_form": "Adverbios comunes: also, too, very, really, just, maybe",
      "title_target": "Common Adverbs: also, too, very, really, just, maybe",
      "description_md_form": "Los adverbios **añaden información** o **matizan** lo que dices. Son palabras cortas que van antes o después del verbo.\n\n- **also** y **too** significan *también*. **also** va antes del verbo: *I also like tea.* **too** va al final: *I like tea too.*\n- **very** y **really** significan *muy* o *de verdad*. Van antes de un adjetivo: *I am very happy.* / *It is really good.*\n- **just** significa *solo* o *justo*: *I just want coffee.*\n- **maybe** significa *quizás*. Va al inicio de la frase: *Maybe I sleep.*\n\nUsa estos adverbios para hacer tu inglés más natural y expresivo.",
      "description_md_target": "Adverbs **add information** or **modify** what you say. They are short words that go before or after the verb.\n\n- **also** and **too** mean *también*. **also** goes before the verb: *I also like tea.* **too** goes at the end: *I like tea too.*\n- **very** and **really** mean *muy* or *de verdad*. They go before an adjective: *I am very happy.* / *It is really good.*\n- **just** means *solo* or *justo*: *I just want coffee.*\n- **maybe** means *quizás*. It goes at the start of the sentence: *Maybe I sleep.*\n\nUse these adverbs to make your English more natural and expressive.",
      "grammar_structure": "also/too + verbo | very/really + adjetivo | just + verbo | maybe + frase",
      "conjugation_table": null,
      "examples": [
        {
          "id": "exm_gex_001",
          "lexeme_id": "also_adv",
          "chunk_id": null,
          "grammar_slug": null,
          "text_target": "I also like coffee.",
          "translation_form": "También me gusta el café.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "also"
          ],
          "audio_url": null,
          "reading_target": null
        },
        {
          "id": "exm_gex_002",
          "lexeme_id": "very_adv",
          "chunk_id": null,
          "grammar_slug": null,
          "text_target": "I am very happy.",
          "translation_form": "Estoy muy feliz.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "very"
          ],
          "audio_url": null,
          "reading_target": null
        }
      ],
      "callouts": [
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "text_md": "**also** va antes del verbo, pero **too** va al final. No digas *I too like tea* — di *I also like tea* o *I like tea too*."
        },
        {
          "type": "common_error",
          "text_md": "**maybe** va al inicio de la frase, no al final. Di *Maybe I sleep*, no *I sleep maybe*."
        }
      ],
      "common_errors_es": [
        "Confundir **also** y **too**: **also** va antes del verbo (*I also like tea*), **too** va al final (*I like tea too*).",
        "Usar **very** con verbos: **very** solo va con adjetivos (*I am very happy*), no con verbos (*I very like it* es incorrecto).",
        "Poner **maybe** al final de la frase: **maybe** va al inicio (*Maybe I sleep*), no al final (*I sleep maybe* es incorrecto)."
      ]
    }
  ],
